    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "THE HESSIAN MATRIX, LOCAL MAXIMA/MINIMA, AND MULTIDIMENSIONAL TAYLOR SERIES",
      "source_page": 1285,
      "source_pages": [
        1285
      ],
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Evolution_appendix6:p005:b12"
      ],
      "source_bboxes": [
        [
          132.0,
          778.0,
          903.0,
          826.0
        ]
      ],
      "provenance_score": 1.0,
      "coverage_repair": "authoritative_chapter_pdf_source_block",
      "content_sha256": "25da61a26d746f6dab3feb56169e795c48a6292cf5e2fb6944a421e327f593a8"
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "In univariate calculus, the local extrema of a function occur when its slope (first derivative) is zero. The multivariate extension is that the gradient vector is zero, so the slope of the function with respect to all variables is zero. A point $ x {e} $ where this occurs is called a stationary or equilibrium point, and corresponds to either a local maximum, minimum, saddle point, or inflection point. As with the calculus of single variables, determining which of these cases is correct depends on the second derivative. With n variables, the appropriate generalization is the Hessian matrix",
      "source_page": 1285,
      "source_pages": [
        1285
      ],
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Evolution_appendix6:p005:b13"
      ],
      "source_bboxes": [
        [
          132.0,
          848.0,
          905.0,
          1011.0
        ]
      ],
      "provenance_score": 1.0,
      "coverage_repair": "authoritative_chapter_pdf_source_block",
      "content_sha256": "465d98c42016729aa75efb564738c939a6c8d86dc4de3bc88fed83e1da515745"
    },
    {
      "type": "derivation",
      "content": "$$ \\mathbf{H} {\\mathbf{X}}[f]=\\nabla {\\mathbf{X}}\\left[\\left(\\nabla {\\mathbf{X}}[f]\\right)^{T}\\right]=\\frac{\\partial^{2}f}{\\partial\\mathbf{x}\\partial\\mathbf{x}^{T}}=\\left(\\begin{array}{c c c}\\frac{\\partial^{2}f}{\\partial x {1}^{2}}&\\cdots&\\frac{\\partial^{2}f}{\\partial x {1}\\partial x {n}}\\\\ \\vdots&\\ddots&\\vdots\\\\ \\frac{\\partial^{2}f}{\\partial x {1}\\partial x {n}}&\\cdots&\\frac{\\partial^{2}f}{\\partial x {n}^{2}}\\end{array}\\right) $$",
      "source_page": 1285,
      "source_pages": [
        1285
      ],
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Evolution_appendix6:p005:b14"
      ],
      "source_bboxes": [
        [
          171.0,
          1040.0,
          790.0,
          1196.0
        ]
      ],
      "provenance_score": 1.0,
      "coverage_repair": "authoritative_chapter_pdf_source_block",
      "content_sha256": "7175b65a89fe51562038b2fb5e932914123f304c6f5e24e7de6d4a1b1a907273"
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "Note that this is the outer product of $ \\nabla {\\mathbf{x}} [f] $ with itself. Recall for an $ n $-dimensional column vector $ \\mathbf{a} {n\\times1} $ that while the inner product, $ \\mathbf{a} {1\\times n}^T \\mathbf{a} {n\\times1} = \\sum a i $, returns a $ 1 \\times 1 $ matrix (a scalar), the outer product, $ \\mathbf{a} {n\\times1} \\mathbf{a} {1\\times n}^T $, returns an $ n \\times n $ matrix whose $ ij $th element is $ a i a j $, or (in our case)",
      "source_page": 1285,
      "source_pages": [
        1285
      ],
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Evolution_appendix6:p005:b16"
      ],
      "source_bboxes": [
        [
          131.0,
          1232.0,
          904.0,
          1327.0
        ]
      ],
      "provenance_score": 1.0,
      "coverage_repair": "authoritative_chapter_pdf_source_block",
      "content_sha256": "e6dcfa78c8a40c996ad5f388f710311c95d68800d831aed510eed20ee7eb13ce"
    },
    {
      "type": "derivation",
      "content": "$$ \\mathbf{H} {ij}=\\frac{\\partial\\left(f(\\mathbf{x})/\\partial x {i}\\right)}{\\partial x {j}}=\\frac{\\partial^{2}f(\\mathbf{x})}{\\partial x {i}\\partial x {j}} $$",
      "source_page": 1285,
      "source_pages": [
        1285
      ],
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Evolution_appendix6:p005:b17"
      ],
      "source_bboxes": [
        [
          383.0,
          1341.0,
          650.0,
          1394.0
        ]
      ],
      "provenance_score": 1.0,
      "coverage_repair": "authoritative_chapter_pdf_source_block",
      "content_sha256": "ff732cd5739c2a2ea1770ce1903ead761a76f5eeb79be3a3006dd92ac154f490"
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "This matrix is symmetric, as mixed partials are equal under suitable continuity conditions, and it measures the local multidimensional curvature of the function.",
      "source_page": 1285,
      "source_pages": [
        1285
      ],
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Evolution_appendix6:p005:b18"
      ],
      "source_bboxes": [
        [
          132.0,
          1422.0,
          903.0,
          1470.0
        ]
      ],
      "provenance_score": 1.0,
      "coverage_repair": "authoritative_chapter_pdf_source_block",
      "content_sha256": "d70f77291a43ce7f3f96e04234d5b29be25a562b90b4ae727e169f475f04051a"
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "where all partials are evaluated at $ \\mathbf{x} o $. If we note that the first sum is the inner product of the gradient and $ (\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{x} o) $, and the double sum is a quadratic product involving the Hessian, we can express Equation A6.7a in matrix form as",
      "source_page": 1286,
      "source_pages": [
        1286
      ],
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Evolution_appendix6:p006:b11"
      ],
      "source_bboxes": [
        [
          287.0,
          927.0,
          1059.0,
          999.0
        ]
      ],
      "provenance_score": 1.0,
      "coverage_repair": "authoritative_chapter_pdf_source_block",
      "content_sha256": "0666af53d8069923798c648431d533eeb941ab8d755e7c2084716ad08050d47c"
    },
    {
      "type": "derivation",
      "content": "$$ f(\\mathbf{x})\\simeq f(\\mathbf{x} {o})+\\nabla^{T}(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{x} {o})+\\frac{1}{2}(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{x} {o})^{T}\\mathbf{H}(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{x} {o}) $$",
      "source_page": 1286,
      "source_pages": [
        1286
      ],
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Evolution_appendix6:p006:b12"
      ],
      "source_bboxes": [
        [
          447.0,
          1018.0,
          899.0,
          1063.0
        ]
      ],
      "provenance_score": 1.0,
      "coverage_repair": "authoritative_chapter_pdf_source_block",
      "content_sha256": "d305d8b6438291f6d3c137851d24198edd0339bd3c07747e5bd7464f63c2551f"
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "where $ \\nabla $ and H are the gradient and Hessian of f with respect to x when evaluated at $ x {0} $,",
      "source_page": 1286,
      "source_pages": [
        1286
      ],
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Evolution_appendix6:p006:b14"
      ],
      "source_bboxes": [
        [
          287.0,
          1078.0,
          1053.0,
          1102.0
        ]
      ],
      "provenance_score": 1.0,
      "coverage_repair": "authoritative_chapter_pdf_source_block",
      "content_sha256": "4bd25feffae0332477cd42ab72553f7bc74eda5ff055558e901d30da7757f557"
    },
    {
      "type": "derivation",
      "content": "$$ \\nabla \\equiv \\left.\\nabla_{\\mathbf{X}}[f]\\right|_{\\mathbf{X}=\\mathbf{X}_o} \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad \\mathbf{H} \\equiv \\left.\\mathbf{H}_{\\mathbf{X}}[f]\\right|_{\\mathbf{X}=\\mathbf{X}_o} $$",
      "source_page": 1286,
      "source_pages": [
        1286
      ],
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Evolution_appendix6:p006:b15"
      ],
      "source_bboxes": [
        [
          461.0,
          1120.0,
          884.0,
          1152.0
        ]
      ],
      "provenance_score": 1.0,
      "coverage_repair": "authoritative_chapter_pdf_source_block",
      "content_sha256": "ab6e58c0d55df2626d381515cde4a6806caaf30bea771ca324f43bf9533cab36"
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "At an equilibrium point, $ \\hat{x} $, all first partials are zero, so $ (\\nabla {\\mathbf{x}}[f])^{T} $ is evaluated at $ \\hat{x} $ is a vector of length zero. Whether this point is a maximum or minimum is then determined by the quadratic product involving the Hessian when evaluated at $ \\hat{x} $. Consider a vector, d, of a small change from the equilibrium point",
      "source_page": 1286,
      "source_pages": [
        1286
      ],
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Evolution_appendix6:p006:b16"
      ],
      "source_bboxes": [
        [
          288.0,
          1172.0,
          1058.0,
          1267.0
        ]
      ],
      "provenance_score": 1.0,
      "coverage_repair": "authoritative_chapter_pdf_source_block",
      "content_sha256": "83d6350fceafd4c0788a5b095bcb6173b4aab1d7e122b9d1871fdad4413d206c"
    },
    {
      "type": "derivation",
      "content": "$$ f(\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}+\\mathbf{d})-f(\\hat{\\mathbf{x}})\\simeq\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot\\mathbf{d}^{T}\\mathbf{H}\\mathbf{d} $$",
      "source_page": 1286,
      "source_pages": [
        1286
      ],
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Evolution_appendix6:p006:b17"
      ],
      "source_bboxes": [
        [
          545.0,
          1283.0,
          803.0,
          1331.0
        ]
      ],
      "provenance_score": 1.0,
      "coverage_repair": "authoritative_chapter_pdf_source_block",
      "content_sha256": "d46a880780675899af21e26bde5dd6530bc7f8951f8c2bf97c20c2dc10353dc7"
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "Because H is a symmetric matrix, we can diagonalize it and apply a canonical transformation (Equation A5.17a) to simplify the quadratic product in Equation A6.8a, which returns",
      "source_page": 1286,
      "source_pages": [
        1286
      ],
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Evolution_appendix6:p006:b19"
      ],
      "source_bboxes": [
        [
          287.0,
          1345.0,
          1058.0,
          1394.0
        ]
      ],
      "provenance_score": 1.0,
      "coverage_repair": "authoritative_chapter_pdf_source_block",
